Yeah, all my code was intended for repairing that specific save file.

Based on what I've seen, I don't think that code of the sort lmfback
suggests would be plausible.  The big problem is that if you only have
the savefile, and not the individual level files, then there would be
no way of knowing where the corrupt level ends and the next one
begins.  It'd just keep reading forever looking for that -1 indicating
the end of an object chain (or whatever delimeter is used for other
data).  And since there are several places where raw data is read
directly into a structure, there's no unique sequence of bytes that
could be used to mark the end of a level independent of the context
in which it appears.  Storing the offsets at the start of the file
would work, but that would break savefile compatibility.

But even if you have the level files, there would be a few issues.
For example, the reason I needed to modify the old level file at all
instead of just playing it as it was was because the Candelabrum was
there. Any code to do this automatically would need to scan the entire
savefile and determine if any necessary objects were missing from
the game. Then the level generating code would need to be modified
to add those objects.  And it'd need a special case for when the
level being generated is the one the objects are *supposed* to be on,
since then they can't just be placed randomly... So this part's
*possible*, but it would be a major change, not just a quick patch.

So with all that in mind, I suspect that time would be better spent
working on the code that creates the savefiles.  If the problem
had been detected there, it might have been possible to determine
where the bug that led to this was.  But since it was restore.c
that called panic, all I can tell is that if part of the savefile
is replaced by random bytes, it won't be restored properly. Not
exactly helpful when it comes to finding the source.  I couldn't
really give any more details about how this might be done, since I
didn't study that part of the code while working on this.

The good news is, I'm fairly certain that the bug was in Vulture's
Eye rather than the nethack core.  Before I started using that
frontend, all the crashes were due to some OS glitch or another,
rather than the game.  I switched to a vanilla nethack build
after finishing my ascension, and I haven't had any problems with
it yet, although it's too early to draw any real conclusions from
that.  All in all, I think the best solution is just to back up
the savefile more often.


